A||||8||JohnnyK||Oil leak from somewhere||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||04-30-2000||06:17 PM||||I noticed that there was a fair bit of oil dripping into my driveway, and upon looking, was pooled up on the bottom of the oil pan (ALl of the bottom, not just near the bolt).. So I thought it might have been leaking through.. Took it off, the inside looks fine, and it doesn't look like its coming fromt the gasket? Since it is pooled up on the oil pan, should I just get a new gasket and oil pan and see if that fixes it? (I bought a gasket set that is cork and rubber, but I heard they are no good.. Is there somewhere I can get a just rubber one? (Partsource of Canadian Tire?) Thanks!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||04-30-2000||07:09 PM||||Oil dripping from the pan bolt will blow around a bit when driving, so be sure you have a good oil pan bolt. <P>A good suggestion is to get a 1/2" nylon washer and place it under the bolt. Solved my mystery oil leak problem and for only $.50.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bruce||04-30-2000||09:06 PM||||Same for me- I replaced the bolt and washer, and no more leak. I went a little further and bought the magnetic bolt to pick up stray metal fragments in the oil pan.||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||04-30-2000||10:07 PM||||Well, I took the oil pan off because I heard that it could rust through.. So, doesn't look like it did, but it is fairly rusty.. Should I just buy a new one? And, can I get an all rubber gasket? Thanks<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||mshill||04-30-2000||10:59 PM||||The cork rubber gasket is sufficient for the oil pan.  Smear a thin coat of Ultra Copper RTV on both metal surfaces to seal the gasket up good.  This combination will not leak if done properly.  I tried RTV only and gasket only and was not satisfied with the seal until I used both.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||05-01-2000||05:32 AM||||Bolt up your oil pan and replace the o-ring on your distributer! It should break apart while you are taking it off if its old and leaking oil. Oil is very sneaky and will drip downward and look like a leak from somewhere else. Let us know what happens!<P>Good luck!||||reg||2
Z||000006||Gokart||05-01-2000||09:19 PM||||A rubber gasket is available for the oil pan. That's what I used when I changed it last fall. Put a very thin bead of rtv in groove of oil pan then put in the gasket and let it set long enough  so it won't move on you when you go to bolt it up. No rtv is necessary on top of the gasket.||||reg||2
Z||000007||JohnnyK||05-01-2000||11:23 PM||||I was told at the store to put a thing layer on the oil pan, and then smear it around with my finger till it is thing, then put the gasket on and let it sort of adhere itself there.. Then, when I'm ready, do the same with the block.. Is this a good idea?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Gokart||05-02-2000||06:21 AM||||The first half sounds like it will have the same effect, but I was told not to put any on the block though when using the rubber gasket.<p>[This message has been edited by Gokart (edited 05-02-2000).]||||reg||1
